🚚 Project: Delivery Management System

Build a console-based Java application that manages different types of deliveries.

Scenario

A logistics company has different types of vehicles:

Cars
Trucks
Motorcycles

Customers can create delivery orders, and the system calculates the delivery cost depending on the type of delivery.

1. Create an abstract Vehicle class

It should contain common information such as:

vehicleId
brand
capacity

And an abstract method:

public abstract void move();

You can also have a normal method:

public void displayInfo()
2. Create three subclasses

Create:

Car
Truck
Motorcycle

All should extend Vehicle.

Each one should override:

move()

For example, conceptually:

Car → "Car is moving"
Truck → "Truck is transporting goods"
Motorcycle → "Motorcycle is delivering"

This gives you inheritance + polymorphism + method overriding.

3. Create a Deliverable interface

Since this is the concept you're currently learning, make this a major part of the project.

public interface Deliverable {

    void deliver();

    double calculateShippingCost(double distance);

}

Notice:

No method bodies.

Your delivery classes must implement them.

4. Create different delivery types

For example:

FoodDelivery
PackageDelivery
DocumentDelivery

Each should implement:

Deliverable

But each calculates its shipping cost differently.

For example, you might decide:

Food:
base cost + distance × rate

Package:
base cost + distance × rate + weight charge

Document:
base cost + distance × rate

You decide the actual numbers.

That's part of the exercise.

5. Use encapsulation

Don't do this:

public double price;

Instead:

private double price;

Then provide appropriate getters/setters.

You've already been working with encapsulation, so this project should make you use it rather than just understand the definition.

6. Create a Customer class

Give customers things like:

customerId
name
phone
address

Keep the fields private.

7. Create a DeliveryOrder class

An order could contain:

orderId
customer
delivery
vehicle
distance

For example:

Order #1001
Customer: Prince
Delivery: Package
Vehicle: Truck
Distance: 25km
8. Use a HashMap

You've already reached HashMaps in your exercises.

Use one to store your orders:

HashMap<Integer, DeliveryOrder> orders;

The order ID should be the key.

You should be able to:

Add order
Find order by ID
Remove order
Display all orders
9. Use an array of vehicles

You've already practiced putting different subclasses into a parent-type array.

Do it again here:

Vehicle[] vehicles = {
    new Car(...),
    new Truck(...),
    new Motorcycle(...)
};

Then use an enhanced for loop:

for (Vehicle vehicle : vehicles) {
    vehicle.move();
}

The important part is that you're calling the same method on different objects and getting different behavior.

That's polymorphism in practice.

10. Main menu

Your Main class should have a simple console menu:

===== DELIVERY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM =====

1. Add Customer
2. Add Delivery Order
3. View Order
4. View All Orders
5. Calculate Shipping Cost
6. Deliver Order
7. Remove Order
8. Exit

Choose an option:

You can use Scanner.

Your constraints 🔥

I wouldn't use Spring Boot for this one.

Pure Java.

And don't use anything you haven't learned yet just because you can Google it.

Try to solve it with the tools you've accumulated so far:

